<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Shirvan Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1748 CE – 1820 CE · capital Shamakhi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A khanate in the old Shirvan heartland around Shamakhi, which rose after Nader Shah's death and was absorbed by Russia in 1820.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah's</a> death in 1747, local leaders in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvan</a> formed an independent khanate around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a>, the old capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> State. Its early years were divided between rival families and two capitals, Old Shamakhi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghsu">New Shamakhi</a>, also called Aghsu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvan</a> was a land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a>, but earthquakes and wars had reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> from its former greatness. The khanate was for a time dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-of-quba">Fatali Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quba-khanate">Quba Khanate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-khan-of-shirvan">Mustafa Khan</a>, who ruled from 1792, reunited it and built a mountain stronghold at Fit-dagh, balancing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-khan-of-shirvan">Mustafa Khan</a> accepted Russian protection in 1805 but remained restless. In 1820 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> abolished the khanate, making <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvan</a> a province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> later became the seat of a Russian governorate until a devastating earthquake in 1859 moved it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shirvan-khanate.jpg" alt="Shirvan Khanate" width="320" height="250"><figcaption>Image: HistoryofIran Based on two maps from Bournoutian, George (2021). From the Kur to the Aras: A Military History of Russ... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_South_Caucasus_in_the_last_quarter_of_the_18th_century.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1748 CE – 1820 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shamakhi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mustafa Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Shirvan, Shamakhi Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1784&amp;focus=shirvan-khanate" aria-label="Shirvan Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5873 726 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shirvan Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6193 982L6298 1144M6193 982L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6193 982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6193 982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Shamakhi. <a href="/atlas?year=1784&amp;focus=shirvan-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
